24 PRACTICAL EXERCISES / JAVA 8 BASELINE
C19
Bounded producer-consumer handoff
Send integers 1 through n from one producer to one consumer, then stop. Return their
sum with a five-second completion bound.
APPROACH - EASY EXPLANATION
Use a bounded queue and a sentinel that cannot be a valid item. Await both task outcomes.
Cancellation and shutdown happen in finally. The protocol here has exactly one producer and one
consumer.
JAVA SOLUTION (REFERENCE METHOD)
static long queueSum(int n) throws Exception {
    if (n < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("n");
    BlockingQueue<Integer> queue = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(4);
    ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);
    try {
        Future<?> producer = pool.submit(() -> {
            try {
                for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) queue.put(i);
                queue.put(-1);
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); throw new RuntimeException(e);
            }
        });
        Future<Long> consumer = pool.submit(() -> {
            long sum = 0; int x;
            while ((x = queue.take()) != -1) sum += x;
            return sum;
        });
        long result = consumer.get(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
        producer.get(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
        return result;
    } finally { pool.shutdownNow(); }
}
Worked result: n=5 gives 15; n=0 gives 0.
Time and space: O(n) work, O(capacity) queue space. Waiting depends on scheduling.
Edge-case policy: The sentinel cannot collide with positive items. More consumers need an expanded
termination protocol.
Original guide coding lab: C19 · source page 141
