24 PRACTICAL EXERCISES / JAVA 8 BASELINE
C20
Exact counter under concurrent increments
Run a chosen number of tasks, each incrementing one exact shared counter a chosen
number of times.
APPROACH - EASY EXPLANATION
Use AtomicLong and wait for every Future before reading the result. Keep the test workload bounded;
in a long-lived production service use an explicit admission policy rather than this exercise factory.
JAVA SOLUTION (REFERENCE METHOD)
static long concurrentCount(int tasks, int increments) throws Exception {
    if (tasks < 0 || increments < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException();
    AtomicLong counter = new AtomicLong();
    ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4);
    try {
        List<Future<?>> work = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int t = 0; t < tasks; t++) work.add(pool.submit(() -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < increments; i++) counter.incrementAndGet();
        }));
        for (Future<?> f : work) f.get(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
        return counter.get();
    } finally { pool.shutdownNow(); }
}
Worked result: 20 tasks with 1,000 increments gives exactly 20,000.
Time and space: O(tasks * increments) atomic updates, O(tasks) future storage.
Edge-case policy: Waiting for tasks is necessary; submission alone does not mean completion. Counter
overflow policy is separate.
