24 PRACTICAL EXERCISES / JAVA 8 BASELINE
C22
Limit simultaneous work using a semaphore
Execute tasks while admitting at most two into a protected section. Report the maximum
observed concurrency.
APPROACH - EASY EXPLANATION
Use permits to bound entry, count entrants with atomics and release only after successful acquire. A
start latch creates simultaneous demand. The test measures the invariant, not throughput.
JAVA SOLUTION (REFERENCE METHOD)
static int boundedConcurrency() throws Exception {
    Semaphore permits = new Semaphore(2);
    AtomicInteger active = new AtomicInteger(), peak = new AtomicInteger();
    CountDownLatch start = new CountDownLatch(1);
    ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(6);
    List<Future<?>> work = new ArrayList<>();
    try {
        for (int i = 0; i < 6; i++) work.add(pool.submit(() -> {
            start.await(); permits.acquire();
            try {
                int now = active.incrementAndGet();
                peak.accumulateAndGet(now, Math::max);
                try { Thread.sleep(10); }
                finally { active.decrementAndGet(); }
            } finally { permits.release(); }
            return null;
        }));
        start.countDown();
        for (Future<?> f : work) f.get(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
        return peak.get();
    } finally { pool.shutdownNow(); }
}
Worked result: The peak is between 1 and 2 after all six tasks complete.
Time and space: O(tasks) coordination work, O(tasks) futures; admission is capped at two.
Edge-case policy: A concurrency cap does not impose a requests-per-second rate. Release belongs after
acquire succeeds.
