import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;
public class Lab19 {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    StableValue<String> holder = StableValue.of();
    AtomicInteger calls = new AtomicInteger();
    try {
      holder.orElseSet(() -> { calls.incrementAndGet();
        throw new IllegalStateException("retryable sample"); });
      throw new AssertionError("missing failure");
    } catch (IllegalStateException expected) { }
    if (holder.isSet()) throw new AssertionError();
    String value = holder.orElseSet(() -> { calls.incrementAndGet(); return "ok"; });
    holder.orElseSet(() -> { throw new AssertionError("already set"); });
    int number=127;
    if (!(number instanceof byte small) || small!=127) throw new AssertionError();
    number=128;
    if (number instanceof byte tooLarge) throw new AssertionError(tooLarge);
    if (!value.equals("ok") || calls.get()!=2) throw new AssertionError();
    System.out.println("calls=2; exactMatch=true");
  }
}
EXPECTED OUTPUT
calls=2; exactMatch=true
HOW THE SOLUTION WORKS
The holder retries after failed creation and remains stable after success. The byte pattern checks exact
representability instead of truncating 128 through a cast.
TRY NEXT
Keep preview opt-in explicit. A failed supplier can have partially performed effects;
holder stability is not remote exactly-once processing.
