import java.util.concurrent.StructuredTaskScope;
public class Lab20 {
  static final ScopedValue<String> TENANT = ScopedValue.newInstance();
  public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
    String result = ScopedValue.where(TENANT,"T1").call(() -> {
      try (var scope = StructuredTaskScope.open()) {
        var a = scope.fork(() -> TENANT.get()+":A");
        var b = scope.fork(() -> TENANT.get()+":B");
        scope.join();
        return a.get()+"|"+b.get();
      }
    });
    if (!result.equals("T1:A|T1:B") || TENANT.isBound())
      throw new AssertionError(result);
    System.out.println(result);
  }
}
EXPECTED OUTPUT
T1:A|T1:B
HOW THE SOLUTION WORKS
The scope is opened inside the binding, so supported children inherit it. The default policy requires successful
child completion; output concatenation follows variable order, not completion order.
TRY NEXT
Inject one child failure and observe cancellation/close on a real JDK 25. Add an API-
supported timeout and an uncooperative task to discuss cleanup limits.
