CHAPTER 24 · CONCURRENT PROGRAMMING TASKS QUESTION 116 / 120
Q116. Count concurrent increments exactly.
✎  SIMPLE ANSWER
Use an AtomicInteger for each indivisible increment, retain every submitted Future and observe
completion before reading the final total. Always shut down the owned executor.
SKETCH THE PATTERN
EXAMPLE / INPUT
4 tasks x 1000 increments
➜
KEY LOGIC
Use an AtomicInteger for each
indivisible increment, retain every
submitted Future and observe
completion before reading the
final...
➜
RESULT
4000
💡 Atomic update prevents lost increments; get supplies completed-task observation.
⏱ TIME & SPACE
Work O(tasks*repetitions); stored Futures O(tasks);
contention cost is workload-dependent.
⚠ INTERVIEW PITFALL
The exercise is bounded, not a throughput benchmark;
stopPool is a shared lifecycle helper.
FOLLOW-UP QUESTION · ANSWERED
When is LongAdder appropriate instead of an exact decision counter?
LongAdder suits high-contention statistics when sum need not be an atomic live snapshot. Exact quotas,
ticket numbers and admission decisions require an AtomicInteger/AtomicLong or a lock guarding the full
invariant.
JAVA 17 · SOLUTION Source Q116 · 14 lines
static int concurrentCount(int tasks,int repetitions) throws Exception {
  if(tasks<1 || repetitions<0 || (long)tasks*repetitions>Integer.MAX_VALUE)
    throw new IllegalArgumentException();
  ExecutorService pool=Executors.newFixedThreadPool(Math.min(tasks,4));
  AtomicInteger count=new AtomicInteger();
  try {
    List<Future<?>> futures=new ArrayList<>();
    for(int i=0;i<tasks;i++) futures.add(pool.submit(()-> {
      for(int j=0;j<repetitions;j++) count.incrementAndGet();
    }));
    for(Future<?> future:futures) future.get(5,TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    return count.get();
  } finally { stopPool(pool); }
}
