pages.fullText:
CHAPTER 24 · CONCURRENT PROGRAMMING TASKS QUESTION 120 / 120
Q120. Bound concurrent work with permits and verify
the peak.
✎  SIMPLE ANSWER
Acquire a permit before counting active work and release it in finally. Track the highest observed
active value with an atomic maximum.
SKETCH THE PATTERN
EXAMPLE / INPUT
20 tasks, permits=3 ➜
KEY LOGIC
Acquire a permit before counting
active work and release it in
finally.
➜
RESULT
observed peak between 1 and 3
💡 Every active increment is inside an acquired-permit lifecycle.
⏱ TIME & SPACE
Work O(tasks); stored handles O(tasks), at most eight
worker threads.
⚠ INTERVIEW PITFALL
Peak may be below the limit because work is tiny and
scheduling varies.
FOLLOW-UP QUESTION · ANSWERED
How would rejection or cancellation before start affect permit ownership?
Acquire permits only inside tasks that actually begin, or track reservation state and release exactly once on
rejection, pre-start cancellation, failure, or completion. A permit leaked during cancellation permanently
lowers usable capacity.
JAVA 17 · SOLUTION Source Q120 · 17 lines
static int boundedPeak(int tasks,int limit) throws Exception {
  if(tasks<1 || limit<1) throw new IllegalArgumentException();
  Semaphore permits=new Semaphore(limit); AtomicInteger active=new AtomicInteger(),peak=new 
AtomicInteger();
  ExecutorService pool=Executors.newFixedThreadPool(Math.min(tasks,8));
  try {
    List<Future<?>> futures=new ArrayList<>();
    for(int i=0;i<tasks;i++) futures.add(pool.submit(()-> {
      permits.acquire();
      try { int now=active.incrementAndGet(); peak.accumulateAndGet(now,Math::max); }
      finally { active.decrementAndGet(); permits.release(); }
      return null;
    }));
    for(Future<?> f:futures) f.get(5,TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    if(active.get()!=0 || permits.availablePermits()!=limit) throw new AssertionError();
    return peak.get();
  } finally { stopPool(pool); }
}
