// Same tenant/id -> hit; different tenant -> separate key.
VERIFICATION AND LIMITS
Use parameter positions so the sample does not depend on parameter-name metadata. Define
canonical input identifiers that cannot create ambiguous composite keys; use a custom
KeyGenerator if necessary. Authorize each access according to actual policy: caching a
response must not bypass fresh permission checks that are required on hits. Test load
invocation count, two tenants and self-invocation bypass. This method assumes the chosen
tenant-level representation is shareable within the authorized scope.
SPRING BOOT + REDIS | CLEAN PREMIUM EDITION 127

C04   /   PRACTICAL EXERCISE
Commit-aware invalidation and its remaining race
GOAL AND SETUP
Evict after a successful data update and reproduce a delayed stale loader. Setup: SQL
transaction; transaction-aware cache manager; separate proxied services
ORIGINAL WORKED SOLUTION
@Transactional
@CacheEvict(cacheNames="products", key="#p0 + ':' + #p1")
public void update(String tenantId, String id, ProductChange change) {
  repository.update(tenantId, id, change);
}
// Configure transaction-aware cache behavior and verify advice path.
// Race to reproduce:
// reader loads old DB data -> writer commits/evicts -> reader caches old
VERIFICATION AND LIMITS
ProductChange and repository are application code. Use integration tests for successful
commit and rollback, then use barriers to force the stale-loader race. Transaction-aware
eviction improves commit alignment but is not proof of strict consistency. Choose a version-
aware protocol or authoritative reads for strict requirements; otherwise document bounded
staleness and repair. Do not add a second deletion with an arbitrary sleep and declare all
races solved.
SPRING BOOT + REDIS | CLEAN PREMIUM EDITION 128

C06   /   PRACTICAL EXERCISE
Basic lease acquisition and ownership-safe release
GOAL AND SETUP
Use conditional SET with TTL and compare-and-delete; state the safety limits. Setup:
StringRedisTemplate; unique random token per acquisition
ORIGINAL WORKED SOLUTION
String key = "lease:job:42";
String token = UUID.randomUUID().toString();
boolean acquired = Boolean.TRUE.equals(strings.opsForValue()
    .setIfAbsent(key, token, Duration.ofSeconds(10)));
String releaseLua = "if redis.call('GET',KEYS[1]) == ARGV[1] then "
    + "return redis.call('DEL',KEYS[1]) else return 0 end";
RedisScript<Long> release = new DefaultRedisScript<>(releaseLua, Long.class);
// Run only for the token owned by this attempt:
strings.execute(release, List.of(key), token);
VERIFICATION AND LIMITS
The exercise demonstrates a basic single-store lease, not universal mutual exclusion. Execute
business work only after successful acquisition and handle release in the proper lifecycle. Test
A expiry, B acquisition and late A release: B's key must survive. Then demonstrate how A could
still perform an external write after its lease expired. Protect the resource with a real invariant/
fencing design where required. Failover, pauses and unknown acquisition outcomes remain
explicit design concerns.
